        {
          "index": 3,
          "title": "Section 01 · Limit meaning",
          "html": "<div class=\"cm-section-divider\"><span class=\"cm-section-num\">01</span><span class=\"cm-section-kicker\">Up next</span><h3 class=\"cm-section-title\">What a limit means</h3></div>",
          "kind": "section",
          "group": "divider",
          "section": "1.2",
          "study_tip": ""
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "title": "Informal definition",
          "html": "<div class=\"cm-slide-role cm-slide-role--lesson\"><span class=\"cm-slide-role-label\">Knowledge Point</span><strong>Build the method</strong><p>A limit describes approach, not necessarily the function value.</p></div><p>\\(\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to c} f(x)=L\\) means: as \\(x\\) approaches \\(c\\), \\(f(x)\\) approaches \\(L\\).</p><p><strong>Key:</strong> the limit can exist even when \\(f(c)\\) is undefined or different from \\(L\\).</p>",
          "kind": "lesson",
          "group": "learn",
          "section": "1.2",
          "study_tip": ""
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "title": "Limit notation cheat sheet",
          "html": "<div class=\"cm-slide-role cm-slide-role--lesson\"><span class=\"cm-slide-role-label\">Reference</span><strong>Read notation fluently</strong><p>Say the limit aloud before computing.</p></div><ul class=\"stem-itemize\"><li>\\(\\lim_{x\\to 3} f(x)=5\\): “as \\(x\\) approaches 3, \\(f(x)\\) approaches 5.”</li><li>\\(f(3)\\) is a <em>separate</em> question (filled dot on the graph).</li><li>Open circle at \\((c,L)\\) often signals \\(\\lim_{x\\to c} f(x)=L\\) but \\(f(c)\\neq L\\).</li></ul>",
          "kind": "lesson",
          "group": "learn",
          "section": "1.2",
          "study_tip": ""
        },
        {
          "index": 6,
          "title": "Section 02 · Value vs limit",
          "html": "<div class=\"cm-section-divider\"><span class=\"cm-section-num\">02</span><span class=\"cm-section-kicker\">Up next</span><h3 class=\"cm-section-title\">Function value vs limit</h3></div>",
          "kind": "section",
          "group": "divider",
          "section": "1.2",
          "study_tip": ""
        },
        {
          "index": 7,
          "title": "Piecewise example",
          "html": "<div class=\"cm-slide-role cm-slide-role--lesson\"><span class=\"cm-slide-role-label\">Worked Example</span><strong>Compare limit and value</strong><p>Simplify on both sides of the hole.</p></div><p>Let \\(f(x)=x+2\\) for \\(x\\neq 3\\), and \\(f(3)=10\\).</p><p>\\(\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to 3} f(x)=5\\) but \\(f(3)=10\\). The limit and the function value <strong>differ</strong>.</p>",
          "kind": "lesson",
          "group": "learn",
          "section": "1.2",
          "study_tip": ""
        },
        {
          "index": 8,
          "title": "Section 03 · Graph limits",
          "html": "<div class=\"cm-section-divider\"><span class=\"cm-section-num\">03</span><span class=\"cm-section-kicker\">Up next</span><h3 class=\"cm-section-title\">Limits from graphs</h3></div>",
          "kind": "section",
          "group": "divider",
          "section": "1.2",
          "study_tip": ""
        },
        {
          "index": 9,
          "title": "Reading limits at x = 2",
          "html": "<div class=\"cm-slide-role cm-slide-role--lesson\"><span class=\"cm-slide-role-label\">Graphical Skill</span><strong>Two-sided approach</strong><p>Left-hand and right-hand must agree for the two-sided limit to exist.</p></div><p>From a graph: trace \\(x\\to 2^{-}\\) and \\(x\\to 2^{+}\\). If both approach the same \\(y\\)-value, \\(\\lim_{x\\to 2} f(x)\\) exists.</p><p><strong>False generalization:</strong> \\(f(1)\\) is <em>not always</em> equal to \\(\\lim_{x\\to 1} f(x)\\).</p>",
          "kind": "lesson",
          "group": "learn",
          "section": "1.2",
          "study_tip": ""
        },
        {
          "index": 10,
          "title": "Section 04 · AP MCQ",
