          "study_tip": ""
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "title": "Left-hand and right-hand limits",
          "html": "<div class=\"cm-slide-role cm-slide-role--lesson\"><span class=\"cm-slide-role-label\">Knowledge Point</span><strong>Build the method</strong><p>Approach from one side only — use superscript − or +.</p></div><p>A <strong>one-sided limit</strong> is the value \\(f(x)\\) approaches as \\(x\\) nears \\(c\\) from the left (\\(x\\to c^{-}\\)) or right (\\(x\\to c^{+}\\)).</p><p>Two-sided limit exists only when left and right limits exist and are <strong>equal</strong>.</p>",
          "kind": "lesson",
          "group": "learn",
          "section": "1.3",
          "study_tip": ""
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "title": "Section 02 · When limits DNE",
          "html": "<div class=\"cm-section-divider\"><span class=\"cm-section-num\">02</span><span class=\"cm-section-kicker\">Up next</span><h3 class=\"cm-section-title\">When limits fail to exist</h3></div>",
          "kind": "section",
          "group": "divider",
          "section": "1.3",
          "study_tip": ""
        },
        {
          "index": 6,
          "title": "Jump at x = 3",
          "html": "<div class=\"cm-slide-role cm-slide-role--lesson\"><span class=\"cm-slide-role-label\">Worked Example</span><strong>Compare one-sided limits</strong><p>Different left and right limits → two-sided limit DNE.</p></div><p>Example: \\(\\lim_{x\\to 3^{-}} f(x)=-1\\), \\(\\lim_{x\\to 3^{+}} f(x)=2\\). Because \\(-1\\neq 2\\), \\(\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to 3} f(x)\\) <strong>does not exist</strong>.</p>",
          "kind": "lesson",
          "group": "learn",
          "section": "1.3",
          "study_tip": ""
        },
        {
          "index": 7,
          "title": "Section 03 · Graph design",
          "html": "<div class=\"cm-section-divider\"><span class=\"cm-section-num\">03</span><span class=\"cm-section-kicker\">Up next</span><h3 class=\"cm-section-title\">Sketching with constraints</h3></div>",
          "kind": "section",
          "group": "divider",
          "section": "1.3",
          "study_tip": ""
        },
        {
          "index": 8,
          "title": "Sketch a function with given limits",
          "html": "<div class=\"cm-slide-role cm-slide-role--lesson\"><span class=\"cm-slide-role-label\">Application</span><strong>Design the graph</strong><p>Open vs closed dots encode limits vs function values.</p></div><p>Sketch \\(g\\) such that: \\(g(3)=-1\\), \\(\\lim_{x\\to 3} g(x)=4\\), and \\(g\\) is increasing on \\((-2,3)\\).</p><p><strong>Tip:</strong> open circle at \\((3,4)\\) for the limit, filled dot at \\((3,-1)\\) for the value.</p>",
          "kind": "lesson",
          "group": "learn",
          "section": "1.3",
          "study_tip": ""
        },
        {
          "index": 9,
          "title": "Section 04 · AP MCQ",
          "html": "<div class=\"cm-section-divider\"><span class=\"cm-section-num\">04</span><span class=\"cm-section-kicker\">Up next</span><h3 class=\"cm-section-title\">AP-style multiple choice</h3></div>",
